	My wizard got to dungeon level 13, and an arch lich showed up.
I slapped it around a bit, and it teleported to the stairs.  I used my
standard cunning plan:  I stood a bit away from the stairs.  It would
move adjacent to me, I would circle around it, get to the stairs,
then slap it around some more.

	(I admit that "stand in one place until the enemy gets bored
enough to attack" is not particularly favored by Conan-esque types.
So sue me:  I was playing a wizard.)

	Unfortunately, the arch lich had his own cunning plan:  
instead of leaving the staircase, he summoned monsters, including 
a disenchanter.  I level teleported away, and came up with an 
Secondary Alternate Backup Emergency Redundant cunning plan:

	I collected a heap of food, sat on the stairs on level 12,
and waited for a cockatrice to show up.

	(I admit that this is a rather lazy approach to the tactical
problem, especially compared to two zaps with a wand of digging and 
two episodes of slapping.  So sue me:  I wasn't feeling energetic.)

	Maybe 5,000 turns later, most of the food was gone, and I was
chased off level 12 by some orcs that I didn't want to kill yet.
(Think "walking food rations".)  Let's see:  the two staircases on 
12 were fairly close together, so my S.A.B.E.R. cunning plan would
still work if I killed a cockatrice on level 11.  

	I sat on the stairs on level 11 and waited for a cockatrice 
to show up.

	(I admit that this was not a particularly exciting approach 
to the tactical and logistical problems, particularly since Marvin 
would have ascended by now and half-finished the next game.  So sue
me:  I was comfortable, OK?)

	Maybe 5,000 turns later, giants showed up.  Greedy for an
early strength of 18/**, I killed one and teleported the other three
away.  Unfortunately, they didn't teleport very far.  I stolidly
finished eating the first giant while another giant was slapping me.  
I then went down the stairs to level 12 and slaughtered the orcs.

	The current cunning plan was now:  sit on the stairs up to 
level 11 until I got hungry.  (If you're hungry, you can safely 
eat two giants in one sitting).  The arch lich would have to wait.

	(I admit that a lot of these cunning plans consist of stay-
ing in one place and doing nothing.  So sue me:  I have patience.)

	Before I got hungry, giants showed up on level 11.  Still
satiated...  three of them...  I still want 18/** strength ASAP...  
not a problem:  I have a cunning plan.

	I began racing around the level with the giants in hot 
pursuit.  

	A few times, I stood there and let two giants slap at me
until the third giant showed up.  This kept the giants together, 
which reduced the risk of them splitting up and coming at me from
both sides of the loop of corridors that I was running around.

	(I admit that the quality of my cunning plans may have
dropped a bit as time went on.  So sue me:  the giants didn't kill 
me, right?  And I actually got off my butt for once!)

	After quite a while of being chased around the level, a 
rust monster showed up.  I teleported away and used ESP to see where 
where everything was.  The rust monster was closing fast, with a 
giant right behind him.  I would have time to take off my rustable 
armor before the rust monster arrived, but I would not have time to 
kill the rust monster before the giant arrived, much less have time 
to put on my armor.  

	Cunning plan:  go for it.

	Therefore, after a long series of events, an equal number 
of cunning plans, and maybe 10,000 turns of sitting on staircases:

   o  I was staying away from level 13 because of the Big Bad
      disenchanter and the Ugly Nasty arch lich;

   o  I was staying away from level 11 because I wanted to build up
      an appetite;

   o  I was wildly running back and forth on level 12 because ditto;

   o  An enraged giant was bearing down on me;

   o  My cunning plan was:  "remove armor, wield a +0 runed dagger,
      and stand there while a giant slaps me around."

	It all made sense at the time, I swear.

